.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt Fugu::StateFile 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Fugu::StateFile
.Nd a small JSON state file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use Fugu::StateFile;

my $store = Fugu::StateFile->new(
    path => '/var/db/myapp/state.json',
    mode => 0600,
)->load;

$store->set(installed => 1);
my $installed = $store->get('installed');
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the file where a daemon and its tools keep the handful of facts
that must survive a restart: a counter, a flag, a timestamp.
It is that file and nothing more.
It holds no PID logic and starts no subprocess:
.Xr Fugu::Pidfile 3p
owns the first, and the caller owns the second.
.Pp
.Fn load
tolerates a missing file and a corrupt one.
A state file that a crash truncated must not stop the program that
would rewrite it.
.Fn save
is atomic, through
.Xr Fugu::File 3p ,
so the file is never the corrupt one that the next
.Fn load
has to tolerate.
.Ss new
.Fn new "%args"
creates a store.
The method does not touch the file.
Call
.Fn load .
.Pp
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "modeXX"
.It Fa path
The state file.
This argument is necessary.
.It Fa mode
The file mode.
The default is 0600, because a state file often holds a secret and a
wider mode is a decision, not a default.
.El
.Ss load
.Fn load
reads the state.
An absent file gives empty state with no error.
A corrupt file gives empty state and records the reason in
.Fn error ,
so the caller can report it and carry on.
The method returns the object either way.
.Ss save
.Fn save
writes the state, with the mode applied before the content.
.Ss get
.Fn get "$key"
returns one value, or
.Dv undef .
.Ss set
.Fn set "$key" "$value"
stores one value and saves.
A caller that changes several keys uses
.Fn data
and
.Fn save
instead of one call for each.
.Ss delete
.Fn delete "$key"
removes one value and saves.
.Ss data
.Fn data
returns the state hash reference itself.
A caller that changes several keys at once changes it and then calls
.Fn save .
.Ss path
.Fn path
returns the state file.
.Ss error
.Fn error
returns the most recent failure.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn load
returns the object.
.Fn save ,
.Fn set
and
.Fn delete
return the object on success and
.Dv undef
on failure, with the reason in
.Fn error .
.Pp
.Fn get
returns a value or
.Dv undef .
.Fn data
returns a hash reference.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example changes two keys with one write:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$store->data->{running}    = 1;
$store->data->{started_at} = time;
$store->save or warn $store->error . "\en";
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn new
dies when
.Fa path
is absent or empty.
That is a programming error.
.Pp
No other method dies.
A failed read and a failed write both report through the return value
and
.Fn error .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr Fugu::Config 3p ,
.Xr Fugu::File 3p ,
.Xr Fugu::Pidfile 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
The store is not shared state.
Two processes that hold the same file both keep their own copy in
memory, and the last
.Fn save
wins.
A process that must not lose a concurrent change reloads before it
writes, or takes a lock of its own.
.Pp
Every value goes through JSON.
A code reference, a file handle, or a blessed object does not encode,
and the
.Fn save
that meets one fails.
